   'description'=>'Set up Freshdesk with a clear ticket workflow, support channels, assignment rules, SLAs, knowledge content, reporting and a safe export plan.',
   'url'=>'/freshdesk/how-to-use/','date'=>'2026-10-28','status'=>'published',
   'content'=><<<'HTML'
<h2>Before you open the admin settings</h2><p>Freshdesk works best when the team agrees on the support process before creating rules. Write down the request types you handle, the agents or groups responsible for them, the customer information you need, and the response targets managers will track. Keep the first setup small enough to review after real tickets arrive.</p>
<p>Freshworks distinguishes standard Freshdesk, which it describes as email-first, from Freshdesk Omni, which supports a broader multichannel workflow. Confirm which product and plan your account uses in the <a href="https://www.freshworks.com/freshdesk/helpdesk-ticketing/">official product and pricing page</a>, checked October 28, 2026.</p>
<h2>1. Confirm your plan and invite the right people</h2><p>Open the account's Plans and Billing area and confirm the product, plan and trial or paid status. Freshworks says new accounts start with a 14-day trial, and the Free Program is limited to two agents for six months on Freshdesk only. These terms were checked October 28, 2026 in the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/50000011778">official pricing and subscription guide</a> and <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/50000010099-explore-freshdesk-free-program-freshdesk-only-">Free Program guide</a>.</p>
<p>Invite only people who need access for the first rollout. Put agents into groups that match real work, such as billing, account access or product support. Agree on who reviews unassigned tickets and who can change automation or SLA settings.</p>
<h2>2. Connect the support channels you can staff</h2><p>Use the Freshdesk onboarding and setup documentation to connect the support address and any other channel included in your plan. Do not turn on every available channel on day one. Start with the channel where requests already arrive, verify that a new customer message creates a ticket, and confirm that the reply reaches the right customer.</p>
<p>If the business needs WhatsApp, Instagram or several channels in a shared workspace, verify whether standard Freshdesk or Freshdesk Omni is the correct product. The <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions">official Freshdesk knowledge base</a>, checked October 28, 2026, groups setup instructions for ticketing channels, portal customization and account configuration.</p>
<h2>3. Define ticket fields and categories</h2><p>Choose a small set of fields that help the team route or report work. Useful fields may include request type, product area, urgency and customer segment, but only add a field when agents can choose it consistently. If the same request could fit several categories, write a short rule for which one takes priority.</p>
<p>Test the form with a new ticket before asking every agent to use it. Check whether required fields make sense for urgent or unusual requests. Too many mandatory fields can slow the first response and encourage inaccurate values.</p>
<h2>4. Create a simple ticket assignment rule</h2><p>Freshdesk documents ticket-creation automations that can assign tickets to groups or agents, set ticket properties, send notifications and mark spam. In the documented interface, administrators open Admin, Workflows, Automations, then the Ticket Creation tab to add a rule. Menu labels may vary by account and product version, so use the current <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/37614-setting-up-automation-rules-to-run-on-ticket-creation">official ticket-creation automation guide</a>, checked October 28, 2026.</p>
<ol><li>Write the rule in plain language first, such as “billing requests go to the billing group”.</li><li>Choose the conditions that reliably identify the request, such as a form value or source.</li><li>Choose one clear action, such as assigning the group or setting priority.</li><li>Preview the rule and confirm the condition matches the intended tickets.</li><li>Create a test ticket and inspect its activity before enabling the rule for all requests.</li></ol>
<p>Start with the most predictable cases. Add more complex rules only after the team sees how the first rules behave. If two rules can match the same ticket, document their order and the expected result.</p>
<h2>5. Set response targets and escalation rules</h2><p>Service-level agreement policies define the target time for a first response or resolution. Freshdesk's official guide says administrators can open Admin, Workflows, SLA Policies, then add or edit a policy. It also documents targets by ticket priority, business or calendar hours, and escalation settings where supported. Check the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/229037">official SLA policy guide</a>, checked October 28, 2026, and verify plan eligibility before relying on a control.</p>
<p>Use a small number of policies that match the actual service promise. A VIP or urgent queue may need a shorter target than a general request, but only create a separate policy if the team can staff it. Check that business hours, holidays and ticket priorities reflect the written support agreement.</p>
<h2>6. Publish knowledge articles for repeat questions</h2><p>Freshdesk includes knowledge-base management guidance in its support library. Start with the questions agents answer most often, then write one article for each clear task. A useful article names the required access, gives steps in order, explains the expected result and tells the reader when to contact support.</p>
<p>Before publishing, check the article from the customer's view. Remove internal-only details, verify every link and make sure the steps match the current interface. Update the article when a setting or policy changes, and link agents to it from saved replies where the workflow supports that pattern.</p>
<h2>7. Review reports against the workflow</h2><p>Use Freshdesk's available analytics and reports to inspect ticket volume, response times, resolution patterns and queues that accumulate work. The reporting options vary by plan, so check the current <a href="https://www.freshworks.com/freshdesk/helpdesk-ticketing/">official plan comparison</a>. Do not judge the support team from one metric. A quick first response can still lead to a long resolution if ownership or escalation is unclear.</p>
<p>Review a small group of tickets with the team each week. Look for repeated reassignment, missing categories, tickets reopened after a reply and questions that could be answered by better knowledge content. Use those patterns to change a rule or training note instead of adding automation without a clear purpose.</p>
<h2>8. Export a sample before a migration</h2><p>Freshdesk documents ticket-list exports to CSV or Excel. The export option requires the correct role and permissions. The official guide says a ticket-list export does not include the entire conversation or historical archived ticket data; use account export when you need a broader record. Read <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225158">How to export tickets</a> and <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225487-how-do-i-export-my-helpdesk-data-">How to export help desk data</a>, checked October 28, 2026.</p>
<ol><li>Open a representative ticket view and select a short date range for the sample.</li><li>Choose CSV or Excel and the fields you need.</li><li>Check the file for ticket IDs, contact fields, custom fields and expected text.</li><li>Use account export for wider history that the ticket-list export does not include.</li><li>Keep the original account available until the destination import and record counts are approved.</li></ol>
<h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2><ul><li>Enabling a channel without an owner who can respond to it.</li><li>Creating overlapping automations before the ticket categories are stable.</li><li>Setting an SLA without matching business hours and escalation ownership.</li><li>Writing knowledge articles that describe a menu from a different product or plan.</li><li>Assuming a ticket-list export includes every reply and archived record.</li><li>Budgeting AI sessions as a recurring monthly allowance without confirmation from Freshworks.</li></ul>
<h2>Troubleshooting a ticket that goes to the wrong place</h2><p>Check the ticket's source, field values, group and activity history first. Compare those values with the conditions in the ticket-creation rules. Confirm that a default mailbox assignment is not setting the group before the automation runs, then test one rule at a time. Freshdesk documents rule conditions, actions and common execution limits in its <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/37614-setting-up-automation-rules-to-run-on-ticket-creation">ticket-creation automation help</a>.</p>
<h2>Next steps</h2><p>Use the <a href="/freshdesk/pricing/">Freshdesk pricing guide</a> to confirm plan and AI costs, the <a href="/freshdesk/review/">Freshdesk review</a> to assess fit, and the <a href="/freshdesk/alternatives/">Freshdesk alternatives guide</a> if the product or plan does not match your workflow. For a direct comparison, read <a href="/compare/zendesk-vs-freshdesk/">Zendesk vs Freshdesk</a>.</p>
